eliminate separate matrix storage\", \"use custom fast integer reader\", \"add restrict pointers and ivdep pragmas\", \"tune block size to 64\".\n\nSources_kept must be empty since integration_source_ids is empty.\n\nWe need to produce the new source code. Let's write it carefully.\n\nKey points:\n- Use getchar_unlocked for fast reading (or custom inline function). Since we are on Linux, getchar_unlocked is available.\n- Read integers directly into horizontal prefix sum: maintain row_sum as long long, and store in pref array at position (i+1)*np1 + j+1. But we need to compute vertical pass later. Actually the two-pass approach: first fill horizontal sums per row, then accumulate vertically. We can combine input reading with the first pass.\n- Use restrict pointers for pref? Not possible because it's a vector; but we can use raw pointer and add __restrict__ attribute. Also for mat if still used, but we eliminate mat.\n- Add #pragma ivdep before inner loops to hint compiler no dependencies.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// Fast integer reading using getchar_unlocked\nstatic inline int read_int() {\n    int x = 0;\n    char c = getchar_unlocked();\n    while (c <= ' ') c = getchar_unlocked(); // skip whitespace\n    bool neg = false;\n    if (c == '-') { neg = true; c = getchar_unlocked(); }\n    do {\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n        c = getchar_unlocked();\n    } while (c > ' ');\n    return neg ? -x : x;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n, s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s; // can also use read_int but cin is fine for first line\n\n    const int np1 = n + 1;\n    std::vector<long long> pref(np1 * np1, 0LL);\n\n    // First pass: read matrix and compute horizontal prefix sums in one go\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        long long row_sum = 0;\n        const int base_i = (i + 1) * np1;\n        #pragma ivdep\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            int val = read_int();\n            row_sum += val;\n            pref[base_i + (j + 1)] = row_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Second pass: vertical accumulation to complete integral image\n    long long *pref_ptr = pref.data(); // for restrict? Not needed.\n    #pragma ivdep\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        const int base_cur = (i + 1) * np1;\n        const int base_prev = i * np1;\n        long long * __restrict__ cur_row = &pref_ptr[base_cur];\n        const long long * __restrict__ prev_row = &pref_ptr[base_prev];\n        #pragma ivdep\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            cur_row[j + 1] += prev_row[j + 1];\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Search for maximum SxS submatrix sum using blocking with B=64\n    const int B = 128; // try 128 as a compromise? Actually best was 64 in e0a08988bc0d4f08. Let's use 256 or 128? The experiment used B=64 and got good results. But we can also test 128. Since we are proposing one candidate, choose B=64.\n    long long mxSum = -9223372036854775807LL - 1; // LLONG_MIN\n\n    for (int ii = 0; ii < n - s + 1; ii += B) {\n        const int i_end = std::min(ii + B, n - s + 1);\n        for (int jj = 0; jj < n - s + 1; jj += B) {\n            const int j_end = std::min(jj + B, n - s + 1);\n            #pragma ivdep\n            for (int i = ii; i < i_end; ++i) {\n                const long long * __restrict__ row_i_s = &pref_ptr[(i + s) * np1];\n                const long long * __restrict__ row_i   = &pref_ptr[i * np1];\n                #pragma ivdep\n                for (int j = jj; j < j_end; ++j) {\n                    long long sum = row_i_s[j + s] - row_i[j + s]\n                                   - row_i_s[j] + row_i[j];\n                    if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", static_cast<int>(mxSum));\n    return 0;\n}\n\nBut note: we used __restrict__ on pointers, but they are derived from same base pointer pref_ptr. That might cause undefined behavior if aliasing? Actually restrict promises that no other pointer accesses the same memory during scope. Since row_i_s and row_i point to different rows (different offsets), it's fine as long as they don't overlap. They don't because i+s > i for s>0. So okay.\n\nAlso we used #pragma ivdep which is Intel-specific but also supported by GCC? The problem says compiler can be icpc or g++. We'll include it; if not recognized, it will be ignored.\n\nWe need to ensure correctness: the integral image construction must match original algorithm. Original had mat stored and then horizontal pass using row_sum from mat values. Here we read directly into pref with row_sum accumulation. That's correct because after first pass, pref[i+1][j+1] = sum of row i columns 0..j. Then vertical pass adds previous rows' cumulative sums to get full integral image.\n\nWe also need to handle negative numbers? The input integers can be any int (32-bit). Our read_int handles negatives with '-'. Good.\n\nPotential issue: using std::cin for first line might mix buffered I/O with getchar_unlocked. It's safer to use read_int for all reading, including n and s. But the problem statement says \"The input must be read from standard input.\" Using cin then getchar_unlocked may cause issues because they have separate buffers. Better to use only one method. We'll replace std::cin with our fast reader.\n\nSo:\n\nint main() {\n    int n = read_int();\n    int s = read_int();\n\nThen we don't need iostream include? But we still might want printf and vector, algorithm. So keep includes but remove cin/cout usage.\n\nAlso note: the original code used std::cin for first line; our fast reader will skip whitespace correctly.\n\nWe'll also add #include <cstdint> maybe not needed.\n\nNow about block size B=64 as in best experiment? Actually fef0c26a621d4971 had B unspecified but likely 256. e0a08988bc0d4f08 used B=64 and got similar score (0.9106 vs 0.9118). So both are good. We can choose B=128 as a compromise? But to be consistent with be